<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>openpv/simshady (short: <monospace>simshady</monospace>) is a
  JavaScript package for simulating shading and photovoltaic (PV) energy
  yield. It integrates local weather data and 3D objects into its
  shading simulation, utilizing Three.js meshes for geometric modeling.
  The package performs shading analysis using a WebGL-parallelized
  implementation of the Möller-Trumbore intersection algorithm
  (<xref alt="Möller &amp; Trumbore, 1997" rid="ref-Möller01011997" ref-type="bibr">Möller
  &amp; Trumbore, 1997</xref>). With the software, users can obtain both
  aggregated and spatially or temporally resolved simulated scenes of
  expected PV yields for further processing.
  <monospace>simshady</monospace> provides both a package for web
  development and a command-line interface (CLI) tool for running
  simulations locally.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The main goal of <monospace>simshady</monospace> is to calculate
  solar radiation yields with respect to shading from surrounding
  objects, which is relevant for calculating the yield of PV system in
  real-world conditions. The yield of PV installations depends on the
  shading from surrounding objects such as houses, trees, and terrain,
  which can be obtained through the ray casting algorithm implemented in
  <monospace>simshady</monospace>. As the software can be used through
  CLI and in the browser, it targets both researchers who want to do
  large-scale shading simulations on a server and researchers who want
  to build interactive solar simulation tools. Hence,
  <monospace>simshady</monospace> fills a gap in the area of detailed 3D
  shading simulation with server- and client-side applications.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>Existing software can roughly be grouped into three groups:
  full-suite commercial design tools, simplified yield calculators, and
  developer libraries.</p>
  <p>Full-suite design tools target professional PV planners and combine
  3D system modeling, component databases, and financial analysis behind
  a graphical user interface. HelioScope by Aurora Solar is a web-based
  design platform that approximates near shading by projecting drop
  shadows from 3D objects rather than performing full ray casting, and
  is offered as a paid subscription
  (<xref alt="Aurora Solar, 2026" rid="ref-helioscope2026" ref-type="bibr">Aurora
  Solar, 2026</xref>). PV*SOL premium by Valentin Software is a Windows
  desktop application that performs detailed sub-module shading analysis
  at cell level, but is restricted to interactive single-project use,
  meaning it requires manual, graphical user interface (GUI)-based
  operation for one project at a time without automation or batch
  processing capabilities
  (<xref alt="Valentin Software GmbH, 2026" rid="ref-pvsol2026" ref-type="bibr">Valentin
  Software GmbH, 2026</xref>). PVsyst is the industry reference for
  bankable yield reports and additionally provides a separately licensed
  command-line tool, PVsystCLI, which is the only existing software that
  combines accurate 3D near shading with batch automation, but at a cost
  of several thousand Swiss francs per year
  (<xref alt="PVsyst SA, 2026" rid="ref-pvsyst2026" ref-type="bibr">PVsyst
  SA, 2026</xref>). The System Advisor Model (SAM) developed by the
  National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR) is open source and exposes a
  Python wrapper (PySAM), but its 3D shading capabilities are limited
  compared to dedicated design tools
  (<xref alt="National Renewable Energy Laboratory, 2026b" rid="ref-sam2026" ref-type="bibr">National
  Renewable Energy Laboratory, 2026b</xref>). Within the ecosystem of
  the open-source geoinformation software QGIS, the Urban Multi-scale
  Universal Predictor (UMEP)
  (<xref alt="Lindberg et al., 2018" rid="ref-LINDBERG201870" ref-type="bibr">Lindberg
  et al., 2018</xref>) plugin contains a tool for shading simulations on
  2D digital surface models
  (<xref alt="Lindberg et al., 2015" rid="ref-LINDBERG2015369" ref-type="bibr">Lindberg
  et al., 2015</xref>) called SEBE (short for Solar Energy on Building
  Envelopes).</p>
  <p>Among the developer libraries, the Python package pvlib
  (<xref alt="Kevin S. Anderson et al., 2023" rid="ref-anderson2023pvlib" ref-type="bibr">Kevin
  S. Anderson et al., 2023</xref>;
  <xref alt="Kevin S. Anderson et al., 2026" rid="ref-Anderson2026_ants2d" ref-type="bibr">Kevin
  S. Anderson et al., 2026</xref>;
  <xref alt="Holmgren et al., 2018" rid="ref-holmgren2018pvlib" ref-type="bibr">Holmgren
  et al., 2018</xref>) offers a large range of functionalities. With
  pvlib it is possible to model shading scenarios where the whole 3D
  scene is not needed. Examples are the possibility to model horizon
  shading from far-away objects like hills, or modeling diffuse
  self-shading or partial module shading, to name a few. However, the
  topic of shading simulation with 3D objects is not included in this
  package. Another Python-based software that enables irradiance
  modeling in two dimensions is pvfactors
  (<xref alt="Anoma et al., 2017" rid="ref-anoma_view_2017" ref-type="bibr">Anoma
  et al., 2017</xref>), which computes view factors between PV rows and
  the ground to capture row-to-row and bifacial shading, but cannot
  represent arbitrary 3D obstacles such as trees or buildings. SoDeLe
  wraps pvlib in an end-user GUI for quick residential estimates and
  likewise omits 3D shading
  (<xref alt="Steinacker et al., 2026" rid="ref-sodele2026" ref-type="bibr">Steinacker
  et al., 2026</xref>). Web-based tools for solar panel simulations,
  such as PVGIS, PVWatts, and RETScreen, provide an accessible means for
  non-technical individuals to estimate energy yields based on
  geographic location and building geometry
  (<xref alt="Psomopoulos et al., 2015" rid="ref-psomopoulos2015comparative" ref-type="bibr">Psomopoulos
  et al., 2015</xref>). These tools account for far-shading from the
  horizon or terrain, or for self-shading derived from the ground
  coverage ratio, but lack the capability to perform shading simulations
  using 3D geometries from nearby objects. RADIANCE
  (<xref alt="Ward, 1994" rid="ref-RADIANCE" ref-type="bibr">Ward,
  1994</xref>) is a collection of tools designed for general light and
  shading simulation and developed in the 1990s. For solar simulation,
  the bifacial_radiance tool
  (<xref alt="Ayala Pelaez &amp; Deline, 2020" rid="ref-Ayala_Pelaez2020" ref-type="bibr">Ayala
  Pelaez &amp; Deline, 2020</xref>) exists as a Python wrapper around
  the original software. In combination, these software packages offer a
  variety of shading methods, including detailed 3D shading simulations.
  Table <xref alt="1" rid="tabU003Atool-comparison">1</xref> summarizes
  the tools by the capabilities most relevant to urban-scale PV
  assessment.</p>
  <table-wrap>
